TITLE_INTRO_SCENE_12=Three days ago, Sophie 
TITLE_INTRO_SCENE_13=caught sight of\nthe photograph 
TITLE_INTRO_SCENE_14=I had carelessly left\non my desk.\nIntrigued, she immediately asked me\nfor an explanation.\nI had to tell her everything.
TITLE_INTRO_SCENE_15=She exclaimed innocently:\n'What a scoop!'\nI immediately told her to forget\nabout it; it was far too dangerous.\nBut her journalistic curiosity\nwon out...
TITLE_INTRO_SCENE_16=Now I had heard nothing from her.\n
TITLE_INTRO_SCENE_17=I had no choice but to resume\nthe investigation. I took advantage\nof a scientific conference to enter\nthe house. Sixty minutes to settle\neverything. Yet as I left my office,\nI felt the dark foreboding that\nI might never return.
TITLE_INTRO_SCENE_1=January 1985\n\nThe beginning of my career
TITLE_INTRO_SCENE_2=Sometimes you get a feeling that\ncreeps over you, warning you not\nto go ahead. And yet, that evening,\nI had accepted.
TITLE_INTRO_SCENE_3=A man knocked at my door. 
TITLE_INTRO_SCENE_4=I was\nsurprised by the anxiety written\nacross his face. I invited him to\nsit at my desk. He then told me a\nvery strange story. 
TITLE_INTRO_SCENE_5=Rainer Gelehrtman, 
TITLE_INTRO_SCENE_6=that was his name,\nsuspected his colleague, Professor\nTcherslawsky, of conducting sinister\nexperiments whenever he was alone.\nFor everyone's sake, he begged me\nto investigate.\n'You'll be well paid,' he said.
TITLE_INTRO_SCENE_7=The money convinced me.\nHe then gave me the key, the address\nand a photograph 
TITLE_INTRO_SCENE_8=of the professor's\nhouse. The moment I saw it, I felt\nas though the Devil himself lived\nthere. But did it matter? Gelehrtman\npaid well, and my wallet was empty.
TITLE_INTRO_SCENE_9=Two days had passed since his visit.\nI learned from the newspapers that\nGelehrtman had disappeared.\nWith nothing left to gain, I decided\nto abandon the investigation I had\nbegun.
